+# Prospective local protocol: WAL snapshot retention after #531 / b72c
+
+## Chronology, ownership, roadmap
+New scientific question: storage/reclamation while a coherent read snapshot remains alive. NOT another crash-order, concurrent-prefix or mixed-SELECT experiment. Closed #531 and all b72c/a61e evidence remain unchanged. Current-main intake 2308b8301d69b7089a2e0636486736ed59b61537; README, governing CURRENT_GOAL, ROADMAP, recent open/closed Issues, open PRs and 138 returned branch names inspected via GitHub MCP. Bounded WAL/checkpoint-reader search found no matching allocation. Parallel #3929/#4037 change other concurrency factors; neither is modified. Unpushed work remains unknown.
+Only new research/coordination/wal_snapshot_retention_531_d90e_v1/; proposed branch research/wal-snapshot-retention-531-d90e-20260922. GitHub tools currently expose only read actions (48 functions); provider search identifies the same installed connector and local gh/docker CLIs are absent. No public Issue/branch/source-freeze/PR is claimed. This protocol and hashes are locally fixed before formal execution; any future Issue is retrospective, not public preregistration. Remote publication limitation is not a scientific FAIL or fleet-wide blocker.
+Roadmap: prior-byte audit -> disjoint construction -> freeze code/plan/auditor/environment -> two serial one-shot batches -> raw-only audit/mutations -> intact archive and additive evidence patch -> remote Issue/PR/checks/main only through available authorized writes. No endless retries and no global ROADMAP completion.
+
+## H
+A count-bounded intent history (two rows, scalar watermark) does not imply a bounded WAL extent when a consumer holds an older SQLite read snapshot while accepted newer intents continue. Returning a serialized in-memory copy of the SAME coherent initial view after ending its read transaction should preserve that historical response while allowing checkpoint/reuse. BEGIN without a first read is a distinct exposure control, not an equivalent initial-state response.
+Aggressive wal_autocheckpoint=1 and journal_size_limit=16384 are held fixed. An SQLITE_OK-like first zero from PASSIVE does not certify that all frames were checkpointed. Successful TRUNCATE after reader release is separately verified.
+
+## T
+Supplied Linux x86_64 execution container, CPython3.13.5, SQLite3.46.1, stdlib only. Docker/OrbStack engine/image identity unavailable. No install, network experiment, GUI/input, model/provider, credentials or shared-runtime change. SQLite WAL/FULL, page4096, ordinary file-backed local storage, busy timeout0, read_uncommitted OFF. Guest CPU/frequency are recorded, not controlled; no speed/latency hypothesis.
+Use complete exact #531 model.py (Git blob24329aaedf166b98a5babf5cfb9c61af7c6f40f0) as vendor531.py unchanged. New fixture adapter persists its states atomically. Original source is not claimed to have this adapter or a production bug.
+Two fresh actors per case: writer owns updates/checkpoints; reader uses read-only connection and query_only. Initialize accepted seq1..3, generation4, history2,3 and retired1. Policies HELD_TX and COPY_RELEASE both acquire one coherent full initial view in a transaction; HELD_TX keeps it open; COPY_RELEASE closes it immediately but keeps exact value bytes. BEGIN_ONLY begins with no SELECT. Writer accepts128 subsequent contiguous intents through the unchanged model, persists only two history rows in every commit, and requests automatic checkpoints after each commit. At totals8/32/128 request PASSIVE checkpoint, capture its raw triple and quiescent file bytes/stat sizes. At128 attempt TRUNCATE before release, capture files, sample reader response, explicitly release reader, then TRUNCATE and capture again. All writer work is barrier-ordered, not a randomized race. Raw snapshots are file copies taken only while actor commands are quiescent; not claimed hardware-atomic backups.
+Three modes x2 repetitions =6 cases;128 writes each =768 new private-model transitions, plus initialization. Two immutable3-case batches, balanced mode order. Child reply/wait bounds3s, outer batch25s. Prior batch must have observed exit0 before next. Existing output/consumed marker refuses replay. Checkpoint busy is an expected result, not a retry reason. Unexpected process/SQL/source/timeout failure stops allocation and keeps partials.
+Construction: disjoint three cases with2/4 writes, excluded. Engineering corrections, if any, retained before formal freeze. No same-case formal retry/replacement/exclusion/tuning.
+
+## D
+PASS_WAL_SNAPSHOT_RETENTION_BOUNDARY_SCOPED requires full6 cases/768 writes/12 actor exits/2 batch exits and independent raw audit, plus at least8 real evidence mutations rejected.
+All writer commits use exact #531 APPLIED, at most2 retained receipts, correct generation/watermark/contents; final semantic writer state identical across modes. Both initial-view modes return exactly the initial state, with zero reader changes and no input authority. BEGIN_ONLY returns final state after first SELECT.
+HELD_TX: WAL apparent extent strictly increases at8/32/128; final extent >16384 and at least4 times the paired COPY_RELEASE extent; each PASSIVE first field0 but checkpointed<log; pre-release TRUNCATE returns busy1 and does not erase WAL. COPY_RELEASE and BEGIN_ONLY: PASSIVE fully checkpoints each measured stage, apparent WAL at every measured stage <=65536bytes, pre-release TRUNCATE succeeds/extent0. After explicit reader release ALL modes TRUNCATE successfully to extent0; final stores reopen coherently and all actual exits0. Source/SQL/order/process/bytes must reconcile. A complete gate miss is FAIL/HOLD by exact gate, not a reason to adjust bounds. Hypothesis PASS does not call indefinite-held transactions resource-safe.
+
+## C
+SQLite documents checkpoint starvation; this is a concrete measured application-adapter tradeoff, not a novel SQLite theorem or vulnerability. The new writer always rewrites metadata/history pages and has bounded payloads; other schemas, workloads and checkpoint policy differ. File st_size is apparent extent, not actual physical storage, total I/O, RSS, or economic cost. st_blocks is diagnostic only. 65536 is a fixture comparison threshold, not a general production cap. Releasing reader cannot reclaim WAL when some other untested reader still pins it. The copied response is historical, not magically current or action authority. No concurrent writer while copying file evidence.
+
+## U
+Six controlled cases are not population reliability or failure frequencies. No timing benefit, model tokens, live task correctness, universal disk bound, power-loss, multiple readers/writers, production adoption or global roadmap claim. No calibrated combined uncertainty u_c/coverage factor k is manufactured. Same-author separate auditor/process is not independent human review. Header/checksum parsing and independently reopened SQLite copies verify retained bytes, not authenticity.
+
+## Variables and units
+|Name|Meaning|SI unit|Definition/condition|Type|
+|capacity|Maximum retained history rows|1|2 in every committed view|nonnegative integer scalar|
+|writes_total|Post-initialization accepted updates|1|8,32,128 in formal observations|integer scalar|
+|generation|Application transition generation|1|initial4; increases once per APPLIED|integer scalar|
+|retired|Retired-through sequence|1|initial1; highest evicted sequence|integer scalar|
+|size|Apparent file extent|1 (byte count, non-SI storage unit)|os.stat.st_size at quiescent barrier|integer scalar|
+|allocated_bytes|Reported allocated storage diagnostic|1 (byte count)|512 times st_blocks; not a gate|integer scalar|
+|checkpoint|busy,log,checkpointed frame fields|1|Raw SQLite pragma tuple; fields interpreted separately|integer vector length3|
+|monotonic_ns|Diagnostic time sample|s, stored in ns|one clock domain; no performance comparison|integer scalar|
+Dimension check: byte extents are compared only with byte bounds; frame counts only with counts. Ns are ordered only within the same monotonic clock. The ratio of two byte extents is dimensionless; no conversion to tokens/latency.
+
+Primary documentation: https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html (checkpoint starvation); https://www.sqlite.org/pragma.html (journal_size_limit,wal_checkpoint); https://www.sqlite.org/fileformat2.html (WAL frames). Retrieved2026-09-22. These documents motivate the expected result, not substitute for local measurement.
